questions.explanation:
Idempotency deals with **duplicates**, not loss. Retries handle loss. Duplicates are handled by making the operation safe to repeat, or by remembering an idempotency key and ignoring repeats:

```js
const seen = new Set(), log = [];
const handle = (id) => seen.has(id) ? log.push(id + ":dup") : (seen.add(id), log.push(id + ":ok"));
handle("m1"); handle("m1");
console.log(log); // ["m1:ok", "m1:dup"]  ← second one is a no-op
```

Stripe's `Idempotency-Key` works this way: retry a charge with the same key and the customer is charged once. The false option mixes up the two jobs. An idempotent handler still needs at-least-once delivery underneath, or a lost message never gets retried at all.
